<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Electorate of Cologne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Archbishopric of Cologne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> was the ecclesiastical principality of the archbishops of Cologne within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, one of its seven original electorates. Besides lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> after the city of Cologne won its freedom, the archbishops ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> from 1180. The state lost its left-bank lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1790s and was dissolved in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/electorate-of-cologne.jpg" alt="Electorate of Cologne" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_St_George%27s_Cross.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> was the Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonia-claudia-ara-agrippinensium">Colonia Agrippina</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s see since Roman times. In 953 the archbishops first gained notable secular power, when Bishop Bruno was appointed duke by his brother, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>. To weaken the secular nobility who threatened his power, Otto endowed Bruno and his successors with the prerogatives of secular princes, which began the electoral state.</p><p>It was formed from the temporal possessions of the archbishopric and came to include a strip along the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> east of Jülich and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> beyond Berg and Mark. It should not be confused with the archdiocese, whose spiritual authority was wider still.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the end of the 12th century the archbishop was one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of the emperor. He was also arch-chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, technically from 1238 and permanently from 1263 until 1803. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Battle of Worringen</a> in 1288 soldiers of the city captured the archbishop and forced him to grant it near-complete autonomy. He later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> to escape conflicts with the city government, and in 1475 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> became a Free Imperial City, independent of him. Bonn was the electors' residence and the electorate's capital from 1597 to 1794.</p><p>In the 16th century two archbishops turned Protestant. Hermann von Wied resigned on converting, but Gebhard Truchsess von Waldburg, who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> in 1582, tried to secularise the archbishopric. The election of a rival, Ernst of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, led to the Cologne War, in which the pope funded Italian and Spanish mercenaries, Bavaria sent troops to Ernst and the Protestant Netherlands backed Waldburg.</p><p>The war ruined most of the electoral economy, and the siege of Godesberg in late 1583 ended with the castle destroyed and most of its inhabitants killed. Waldburg gave up his claim, and Ernst's victory was the first major success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From 1583 to 1761 the archbishopric was effectively a secundogeniture of the Bavarian branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>French forces occupied the left bank territories from 1794 and formally annexed them in 1801. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Reichsdeputationshauptschluss</a> of 1803 secularised the rest, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> to the Landgraviate of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> and Vest Recklinghausen to the Duke of Arenberg. Cologne was re-established as the seat of a Catholic archbishop in 1824 and is an archdiocese still.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Electorate of Cologne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…his small territories west of the Rhine to revolutionary France, and in the Imperial Recess of 1803 he was compensated with the Duchy of Westphalia, formerly ruled by the <b>Archbishop-Electors of Cologne</b>, together with lands of the Archbishopric of Mainz and the Electoral Palatinate on the right bank.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
